1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,440
go and start selling in a loss because one firm enters and gets 5% of the market, what

2
00:00:06,440 --> 00:00:08,960
do your losses look like compared to the entrance losses?

3
00:00:08,960 --> 00:00:14,440
At least 19 times as large.

4
00:00:14,440 --> 00:00:18,120
So if you are the huge firm and you say I'm going to sell everything in a loss to drive

5
00:00:18,120 --> 00:00:23,200
you out of business, if the entrant is small and you're big, you are losing way more money

6
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:24,920
in absolute terms than he is.

7
00:00:24,920 --> 00:00:31,440
It's even worse than it seems because if you go and cut your price, then what will normally

8
00:00:31,440 --> 00:00:34,680
happen to your market share?

9
00:00:34,680 --> 00:00:35,680
Goes up.

10
00:00:35,680 --> 00:00:38,360
So then it's actually even more lopsided.

11
00:00:38,360 --> 00:00:45,080
You gain market share and every percentage point of that market share is poison.

12
00:00:45,080 --> 00:00:48,880
You are losing money.

13
00:00:48,880 --> 00:00:54,240
So anyway, this less formal literature just emphasizes that predation is especially costly

14
00:00:54,240 --> 00:00:55,800
for a monopolist.

15
00:00:55,800 --> 00:00:57,960
But here, notice there's nothing like that.

16
00:00:57,960 --> 00:01:06,800
Here it's just saying that predation is more expensive than accommodation.

17
00:01:06,800 --> 00:01:11,920
Now if you want to go and make this story sound more believable, what you normally want

18
00:01:11,920 --> 00:01:18,320
to do is say well look, obviously nobody wants to predate in the short run, but there is

19
00:01:18,320 --> 00:01:22,680
a long run benefit for predation, which is that you build up a reputation for predation

20
00:01:22,680 --> 00:01:24,880
so people don't enter.

21
00:01:24,880 --> 00:01:29,560
So what if this predation game is infinitely repeated, for example?

22
00:01:29,560 --> 00:01:33,000
So then predation is going to be potentially sustainable.

23
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:37,800
Then it all depends on whether the short run cost of predation exceeds the present value

24
00:01:37,800 --> 00:01:42,200
of the extra monopoly profits you get as a result of predating.

25
00:01:42,200 --> 00:01:48,560
Right in here, all of the classic arguments of the good industrial organization teachers

26
00:01:48,560 --> 00:01:51,080
that you may or may not have had come into their own.

27
00:01:51,080 --> 00:01:56,560
Now these are arguments along the lines of, well look, you have to lose money, you have

28
00:01:56,560 --> 00:02:05,280
to lose 19 times as much as your competitor for a year or two, and then you have to make

29
00:02:05,280 --> 00:02:09,280
it up before another entry comes along and makes you do the same thing.

30
00:02:09,280 --> 00:02:12,760
And then there's other arguments like, suppose you're selling a durable good.

31
00:02:12,760 --> 00:02:16,640
What might your customers do during the period when you are selling below cost?

32
00:02:16,640 --> 00:02:20,040
Buy up and stockpile the good.

33
00:02:20,040 --> 00:02:24,320
Or what might, the entrant might actually say, you know what, I'm going to stop production

34
00:02:24,320 --> 00:02:26,800
and just buy your product and stockpile it.

35
00:02:26,800 --> 00:02:28,800
And I'm just going to sit on it.

36
00:02:28,800 --> 00:02:30,520
And eventually you're going to give up.

37
00:02:30,520 --> 00:02:34,480
And then I'm going to go and sell your own product that you sold to me for below cost

38
00:02:34,480 --> 00:02:35,480
to customers.

39
00:02:35,480 --> 00:02:38,480
You can do that.

40
00:02:38,480 --> 00:02:39,480
Okay.

41
00:02:39,480 --> 00:02:42,320
Now, so questions about that?

42
00:02:42,320 --> 00:02:44,720
Anyone wondering about that?

43
00:02:44,720 --> 00:02:49,040
So just to let you know, so the standard vertical and standard oil, the standard oil did not

44
00:02:49,040 --> 00:02:52,320
practice predation, contrary to what Ida Charbel said.

45
00:02:52,320 --> 00:02:53,320
Right, Ida Charbel?

46
00:02:53,320 --> 00:02:54,320
Anyone know the back story?

47
00:02:54,320 --> 00:02:59,600
What was the deal with Ida Charbel anyway?

48
00:02:59,600 --> 00:03:05,720
Her dad had lost his business and blamed standard oil, and then she goes and writes a book about

49
00:03:05,720 --> 00:03:07,280
how bad standard oil is.

50
00:03:07,280 --> 00:03:11,320
Normally this would at least be mentioned when you are conveying history.

51
00:03:11,320 --> 00:03:16,480
At least when I was taught American history, Ida Charbel's claims were presented as fact.

52
00:03:16,480 --> 00:03:19,360
Even though she did have an obvious ax to grind.

53
00:03:19,360 --> 00:03:24,160
It doesn't mean she's wrong, but it certainly seemed wrong not to tell the students about

54
00:03:24,160 --> 00:03:25,160
this conflict of interest.

55
00:03:25,160 --> 00:03:29,160
And it wasn't done, and I'm bitter about it.

56
00:03:29,160 --> 00:03:30,160
All right.

57
00:03:30,160 --> 00:03:35,840
Now, what does seem to be true is that for a while Rockefeller would buy out his competitors

58
00:03:35,840 --> 00:03:38,680
at above market prices.

59
00:03:38,680 --> 00:03:43,480
So Rockefeller did try getting a monopoly not through predation, which he considered

60
00:03:43,480 --> 00:03:46,040
to be a ridiculous strategy all along.

61
00:03:46,040 --> 00:03:50,480
Instead he just tried going to firms and paying them more than the cost of the refineries

62
00:03:50,480 --> 00:03:53,120
for the refineries.

63
00:03:53,120 --> 00:03:57,400
And tried that for a while, and then eventually he gave up on that too.

64
00:03:57,400 --> 00:04:02,880
And I think there's actually a letter from him saying, well, it was just like blackmail,

65
00:04:02,880 --> 00:04:06,160
where as soon as you paid it, then people started building refineries purely to be bought

66
00:04:06,160 --> 00:04:07,160
out.

67
00:04:07,160 --> 00:04:10,320
So I'm not going to do that.

68
00:04:10,320 --> 00:04:15,880
And so his market share had declined quite a bit, and then even long before the antitrust

69
00:04:15,880 --> 00:04:19,200
case, which dragged on for a long time.

70
00:04:19,200 --> 00:04:22,440
By the way, do you know, like what product was Standard Oil even selling?

71
00:04:22,440 --> 00:04:26,000
So yes, it was oil refinery, but this was before the automobile.

72
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:27,560
Kerosene for lighting.

73
00:04:27,560 --> 00:04:32,520
So I think this story also is very misleading when you tell it to students today, because

74
00:04:32,520 --> 00:04:36,480
students assume that it was being used for automobile fuel, and they think about one

75
00:04:36,480 --> 00:04:41,520
firm having a monopoly or near monopoly over gasoline, and that sounds really scary.

76
00:04:41,520 --> 00:04:44,560
And then they're like, well, wait, no, it was just kerosene.

77
00:04:44,560 --> 00:04:47,160
At a time when the light bulb was spreading.

78
00:04:47,160 --> 00:04:50,840
So it doesn't actually sound all that scary at all.

79
00:04:50,840 --> 00:04:56,680
In fact, it sounds kind of like having a monopoly over like giant bulky desktop computers when

80
00:04:56,680 --> 00:04:59,600
tablets were coming along or something like that.

81
00:04:59,600 --> 00:05:02,520
All right, so questions about predation?

82
00:05:02,520 --> 00:05:03,520
All right.

83
00:05:03,520 --> 00:05:08,280
But anyway, you can go and take those payoffs, and then you can go and do the repeated game

84
00:05:08,280 --> 00:05:11,640
calculation just like I was showing you last week.

85
00:05:11,640 --> 00:05:15,800
And then you'll be able to find a critical beta where it would be worth practicing predation

86
00:05:15,800 --> 00:05:20,280
in order to, as long as the benefits actually were not.

87
00:05:20,280 --> 00:05:22,560
Oh, I have a question.

88
00:05:22,560 --> 00:05:29,120
I had thought that, for instance, in the railroad industry, the idea of the predation would

89
00:05:29,120 --> 00:05:33,240
be like if you had a huge network, rail network, right?

90
00:05:33,240 --> 00:05:39,160
And someone came along with a small point-to-point sort of rail network, you would drop the price

91
00:05:39,160 --> 00:05:44,440
on that single line, and then when they went out of business, then you use the profits

92
00:05:44,440 --> 00:05:45,440
from the rails.

93
00:05:45,440 --> 00:05:46,440
Yeah, yeah.

94
00:05:46,440 --> 00:05:49,280
So that is more, you are correct, that is a more sophisticated version of saying that

95
00:05:49,280 --> 00:05:51,520
you might just go and do that in one market.

96
00:05:51,520 --> 00:05:55,120
Although, that actually leads us right into the next question, which is why assume that

97
00:05:55,120 --> 00:05:57,680
the entrant is small to begin with?

98
00:05:57,680 --> 00:06:00,960
Why might it not be a big firm that's going to go and try to compete with you in a bunch

99
00:06:00,960 --> 00:06:02,040
of places?

100
00:06:02,040 --> 00:06:04,680
And then that strategy does not work.

101
00:06:04,680 --> 00:06:07,800
It doesn't have the same advantage.

102
00:06:07,800 --> 00:06:11,720
So general question, so why can't two play the predation game?

103
00:06:11,720 --> 00:06:15,360
So why can't an entrant predate against incumbents the same way that incumbents predate against

104
00:06:15,360 --> 00:06:16,360
entrants?

105
00:06:16,360 --> 00:06:21,640
And again, the natural solution, again, is think about it in terms of strategies.

106
00:06:21,640 --> 00:06:27,800
You say, look, there's, both firms could practice predation, in which case they both lose money.

107
00:06:27,800 --> 00:06:28,800
Or one does, one doesn't.

108
00:06:28,800 --> 00:06:34,320
And then in that case, the firm that is threatening predation gets a monopoly, and the other one

109
00:06:34,320 --> 00:06:35,520
gets nothing.

110
00:06:35,520 --> 00:06:39,800
Or maybe they're both so scared that they're going to get in over their heads that they

111
00:06:39,800 --> 00:06:40,800
don't enter at all.

112
00:06:40,800 --> 00:06:49,080
But essentially, this is a case where the potential monopoly profits from here balance

113
00:06:49,080 --> 00:06:53,840
out the losses of what's sometimes called destructive or dog-eat-dog competition, where

114
00:06:53,840 --> 00:06:58,280
you get two firms that are competing very heavily in an industry where there's some

115
00:06:58,280 --> 00:06:59,280
costs.

116
00:06:59,280 --> 00:07:05,280
And then things work out badly for both of them.

117
00:07:05,280 --> 00:07:11,680
And furthermore, if people have thought a bit more about the logic of predation, consider

118
00:07:11,680 --> 00:07:12,680
this.

119
00:07:12,680 --> 00:07:17,200
So with predation, as I say, if you are much bigger than your competitor, then you're actually

120
00:07:17,200 --> 00:07:19,920
losing a lot more money in absolute terms.

121
00:07:19,920 --> 00:07:25,480
Now suppose that rather than competing with low prices or selling below costs, you were

122
00:07:25,480 --> 00:07:28,760
to go and try suing them.

123
00:07:28,760 --> 00:07:34,560
Right now, the cost for a really big firm to sue a really small firm, does the big firm

124
00:07:34,560 --> 00:07:38,640
have to pay proportionately more for the legal costs?

125
00:07:38,640 --> 00:07:39,640
No.

126
00:07:39,640 --> 00:07:42,960
So that seems like a much more reasonable way to try to practice predation is through

127
00:07:42,960 --> 00:07:45,920
the legal system, where you just go and threaten your competitors with lawsuits.

128
00:07:45,920 --> 00:07:49,800
And they can threaten you back, but if you are big and they are small, then the lawsuit

129
00:07:49,800 --> 00:07:54,280
would be a much smaller percentage of your profits than of theirs.

130
00:07:54,280 --> 00:07:55,280
So that might be a good thing.

131
00:07:55,280 --> 00:07:57,080
Isn't that kind of what's going on in the tech industry?

132
00:07:57,080 --> 00:07:58,080
Yeah, yeah.

133
00:07:58,080 --> 00:08:03,480
This would be like a great model of being a patent troll, for example.

134
00:08:03,480 --> 00:08:09,440
So of course, you can be a small patent troll, who just, all you do is just sue people for

135
00:08:09,440 --> 00:08:10,440
it.

136
00:08:10,440 --> 00:08:13,960
But the more obvious one is to be a big firm, and any time someone tries competing with

137
00:08:13,960 --> 00:08:17,920
you, you sue them and say, you've infringed our patent.

138
00:08:17,920 --> 00:08:21,560
The single best argument I've ever seen against patents is a single diagram that Alex Tabarrok

139
00:08:21,560 --> 00:08:26,000
made of all the lawsuits of every major firm in Silicon Valley against every other major

140
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:27,000
firm.

141
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:29,880
And it's just like a giant swarm of arrows.

142
00:08:29,880 --> 00:08:34,200
So you just look at that and say, this is your system for increasing innovation.

143
00:08:34,200 --> 00:08:35,200
Everyone sue everyone.

144
00:08:35,200 --> 00:08:36,200
It seems hard to believe.

145
00:08:36,200 --> 00:08:45,840
I know it's just a diagram, but it's empirical in the sense that these are all actual ongoing

146
00:08:45,840 --> 00:08:46,840
lawsuits.

147
00:08:46,840 --> 00:08:52,720
And we said, my God, everyone is, almost everyone is suing almost everybody else.

148
00:08:52,720 --> 00:08:59,000
So anyway, this mixed strategy equilibrium seems to make a lot of sense to me, because

149
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:01,760
there's no way that you can credibly commit to saying, look, I'm the one that gets to

150
00:09:01,760 --> 00:09:03,760
predate, and you're the one that gets predated upon.

151
00:09:03,760 --> 00:09:06,320
Or the other one would say, no, no, no, I'm the firm that gets to predate, and you're

152
00:09:06,320 --> 00:09:07,320
going to get predated upon.

153
00:09:07,320 --> 00:09:14,560
And this story says, look, the bigger the benefits of being the monopolist if you win,

154
00:09:14,560 --> 00:09:18,320
the greater the likelihood that firms will be, or the greater the risk of losing money

155
00:09:18,320 --> 00:09:21,240
that firms will be willing to accept.

156
00:09:21,240 --> 00:09:25,320
So if this monop- so you can go and solve for this yourselves, but intuitively, if these

157
00:09:25,320 --> 00:09:36,440
monopoly profits are really big, then in order to scare- then these losses are going to be

158
00:09:36,440 --> 00:09:37,680
there to balance things out.

159
00:09:37,680 --> 00:09:43,920
In equilibrium, it needs to be the case that you are essentially breaking even.

160
00:09:43,920 --> 00:09:46,920
As you will see.

161
00:09:46,920 --> 00:09:47,920
All right.

162
00:09:47,920 --> 00:09:48,920
Questions?

163
00:09:48,920 --> 00:09:49,920
Questions?

164
00:09:49,920 --> 00:09:50,920
All right.

165
00:09:50,920 --> 00:09:54,400
So now, last topic for this week's notes, although then we'll immediately move on to

166
00:09:54,400 --> 00:09:56,960
the next week, is Bertrand and Curnow competition.

167
00:09:56,960 --> 00:10:01,400
How many of you have heard of these two famous 19th century French anonymous?

168
00:10:01,400 --> 00:10:02,400
So yes.

169
00:10:02,400 --> 00:10:03,400
All right.

170
00:10:03,400 --> 00:10:08,840
So all the arguments that I've been making so far rely heavily on what we call Bertrand

171
00:10:08,840 --> 00:10:09,840
competition.

172
00:10:09,840 --> 00:10:14,160
And to some degree, it also depends upon constant marginal cost pricing.

173
00:10:14,160 --> 00:10:16,800
There is a sub-literature on how much that matters.

174
00:10:16,800 --> 00:10:20,520
But anyway, not that much.

175
00:10:20,520 --> 00:10:26,280
So in Bertrand models in general, the way it works is firms reserve- firms propose prices,

176
00:10:26,280 --> 00:10:30,280
all customers buy from the lowest priced firm, and if the price are equal, then customers

177
00:10:30,280 --> 00:10:31,280
randomize.

178
00:10:31,280 --> 00:10:37,080
And then in the setup, in equilibrium, the most productively efficient firm takes over

179
00:10:37,080 --> 00:10:42,360
the whole market, and they charge a price just below the- just below- excuse me, this

180
00:10:42,360 --> 00:10:46,760
is actually just below the cost, just below the marginal cost of the second most efficient

181
00:10:46,760 --> 00:10:47,760
firm.

182
00:10:47,760 --> 00:10:52,600
And then you can get price equal to marginal cost if these two firms can produce in the

183
00:10:52,600 --> 00:10:54,600
most productively efficient way.

184
00:10:54,600 --> 00:10:56,040
All right.

185
00:10:56,040 --> 00:11:00,960
Now the Bertrand competition strongly undermines the perfectly competitive model.

186
00:11:00,960 --> 00:11:04,320
Because remember, the perfectly competitive model says that you need to have how many

187
00:11:04,320 --> 00:11:06,800
firms to get price equal to marginal cost?

188
00:11:06,800 --> 00:11:07,800
Infinite.

189
00:11:07,800 --> 00:11:11,320
Infinite is the technical number.

190
00:11:11,320 --> 00:11:15,960
And now Bertrand comes along and says, actually, infinity is more than you need.

191
00:11:15,960 --> 00:11:17,800
The right number is two.

192
00:11:17,800 --> 00:11:21,120
All right, so which is of course less than infinity.

193
00:11:21,120 --> 00:11:22,120
Right?

194
00:11:22,120 --> 00:11:26,400
And if you were paying attention, the connectability model says, you know how many you really need?

195
00:11:26,400 --> 00:11:27,400
One.

196
00:11:27,400 --> 00:11:29,000
So wow, all right.

197
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:32,400
So one firm can be just as good as perfect competition.

198
00:11:32,400 --> 00:11:35,720
All right, which is a pretty impressive result.

199
00:11:35,720 --> 00:11:40,280
Especially if you had a crummy undergraduate textbook like I always did, where they said,

200
00:11:40,280 --> 00:11:44,440
look, oh, we got like the- the more firms there are, the better the market outcome.

201
00:11:44,440 --> 00:11:45,440
The end.

202
00:11:45,440 --> 00:11:46,440
Right?

203
00:11:46,440 --> 00:11:50,520
And most of the textbooks that I had never even mentioned, that there might be some conflict

204
00:11:50,520 --> 00:11:55,200
between having- realizing economies of scale and having lots of firms.

205
00:11:55,200 --> 00:12:00,200
So again, like if you- like taken literally, the textbooks appear to be saying that if

206
00:12:00,200 --> 00:12:04,440
we would just go and trust bust every firm down to the atomic level, that this would

207
00:12:04,440 --> 00:12:05,440
be fantastic.

208
00:12:05,440 --> 00:12:06,440
All right.

209
00:12:06,440 --> 00:12:13,800
Ralph Nader in the 70s proposed some legislation which would- which would have put like a maximum

210
00:12:13,800 --> 00:12:18,560
level of concentration at- I think it was, let's see, I think like maximum firm size

211
00:12:18,560 --> 00:12:22,680
was going to be capped at 12.5- 12.5% market share for every industry.

212
00:12:22,680 --> 00:12:27,360
There was one term saying, unless this would lead to significant cost increases.

213
00:12:27,360 --> 00:12:32,640
So but the very fact that he put this in at the end as like a special case, suggests that

214
00:12:32,640 --> 00:12:35,400
it's going to be interpreted unfavorably if it passes.

215
00:12:35,400 --> 00:12:38,520
This did not pass.

216
00:12:38,520 --> 00:12:42,800
There would have remained one good legal remedy, which is just to argue that you're in the

217
00:12:42,800 --> 00:12:47,480
industry for goods and therefore nobody has 12.5% market share.

218
00:12:47,480 --> 00:12:52,240
Right, because if you know anything about any kind of actual anti-drug litigation, your

219
00:12:52,240 --> 00:12:56,240
goal is just to- the goal of the defendant is to find the market as broadly as possible

220
00:12:56,240 --> 00:12:59,080
so that your market share is trivial.

221
00:12:59,080 --> 00:13:00,080
So let's see.

222
00:13:00,080 --> 00:13:04,680
So Visa, there was an interest case against Visa and they had some really good lawyers.

223
00:13:04,680 --> 00:13:09,480
So their lawyers successfully argued that Visa was in what market?

224
00:13:09,480 --> 00:13:15,760
No, no, no, better, better, better than all credit cards.

225
00:13:15,760 --> 00:13:17,920
Better than all banking.

226
00:13:17,920 --> 00:13:20,240
All payments, all payment methods.

227
00:13:20,240 --> 00:13:24,320
All right, so that was a fine team of lawyers.

228
00:13:24,320 --> 00:13:28,480
All right, so they were like, it's just like anything.

229
00:13:28,480 --> 00:13:30,960
Like also burger is included.

230
00:13:30,960 --> 00:13:34,680
You know, someone showing up with a bag of rice saying, what can I get for this?

231
00:13:34,680 --> 00:13:37,680
This is competing with Visa at the rice store.

232
00:13:37,680 --> 00:13:38,680
All right.

233
00:13:38,680 --> 00:13:45,680
So anyway, that is actual litigation and how it works if you're good.

234
00:13:45,680 --> 00:13:46,680
Okay.

235
00:13:46,680 --> 00:13:50,800
All right, now, so this is what the Bertrand model implies.

236
00:13:50,800 --> 00:13:55,960
And in my experience, economists who are very happy with the Bertrand model at first when

